\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Quantum error correction (QEC) protects logical information by encoding it redundantly across many physical qubits and repeatedly measuring stabilizer operators, whose outcomes form a \emph{syndrome}~\cite{shor1995scheme,nielsen2010quantum,terhal2015quantum}. Topological codes---most prominently Kitaev's toric and surface codes~\cite{kitaev2003fault,dennis2002topological,fowler2012surface}---are the leading route to scalable fault tolerance, and recent hardware has demonstrated that scaling such a code suppresses the logical error rate below the physical one~\cite{google2023suppressing}. The component that turns a syndrome into a correction is the \emph{decoder}, and it is the part of the stack where classical algorithms and, increasingly, machine learning meet the physics of the device. Its quality, not the code alone, sets how much protection a given lattice actually delivers.

The incumbent decoders form a remarkably uniform family. Minimum-weight perfect matching (MWPM)~\cite{dennis2002topological,fowler2012surface,higgott2022pymatching}, built on Edmonds' blossom algorithm~\cite{edmonds1965paths}, is the standard for surface codes; belief propagation (BP) and BP with ordered-statistics decoding underpin quantum low-density-parity-check decoding~\cite{poulin2008iterative,panteleev2021degenerate,roffe2020decoding}; and a large, fast-growing literature trains neural networks and reinforcement-learning agents to decode~\cite{torlai2017neural,varsamopoulos2017decoding,baireuther2018machine,krastanov2017deep,sweke2020reinforcement,andreasson2019quantum}, with very recent work exceeding matching accuracy on real hardware syndromes~\cite{bausch2024learning}. What unites them is the objective: each is engineered to approximate the minimum-weight coset leader, and each is benchmarked almost exclusively by the logical error rate under i.i.d.\ (depolarizing or bit-flip) noise. The minimum-weight leader is the maximum-likelihood recovery---but only when the channel is i.i.d.

Two facts about deployment break that premise. First, hardware noise is not i.i.d.: it is \emph{structured}---dominated by one Pauli type (bias), spatially bursty, correlated between neighbours, and accompanied by faulty syndrome extraction. An i.i.d.-tuned decoder forfeits its optimality the moment structure is present, and only a single decoder family tested across regimes on one footing reveals by how much. Second, a decoder inside a fault-tolerant control loop need not commit to every syndrome: it can \emph{abstain}, flagging a shot as unreliable so that a slower fallback decoder, a repeated measurement, or a higher-level protocol takes over. Abstention is the QEC analogue of \emph{selective prediction} in machine learning~\cite{chow1970optimum,elyaniv2010foundations,geifman2017selective}, and it is useful only if the confidence that gates it is \emph{calibrated}~\cite{guo2017calibration,naeini2015obtaining}. These two axes---coverage and calibration---are routinely absent from decoder benchmarks, even though both are first-class concerns for any classifier deployed under uncertainty.

On a distance-$d$ repetition code, eight otherwise dissimilar decoders---optimal lookup, majority, matching, belief propagation, a learned neural coset selector, a topology decoder, a confidence-fused decoder, and a reinforcement-learning selector---are \emph{statistically indistinguishable} on raw logical error (Sec.~\ref{sec:headline}). We prove why (Secs.~\ref{sec:theory_decoders} and~\ref{sec:theory_tie}): every one targets the minimum-weight coset leader, which is maximum-likelihood \emph{only} for the i.i.d.\ channel, so on the line they collapse to the same decision rule. The tie is therefore not a ceiling on achievable accuracy but the signature of a shared blind spot---these decoders ignore the \emph{structure} of the noise.

We introduce the decoder that does not. The \emph{spectral} decoder casts decoding on the code's defect graph and represents the structured channel by a \emph{truncated transfer operator}---an inhomogeneous first-order Markov (classical matrix-product) model whose log-likelihood is a sum of local terms read along the chain---estimated from a small calibration sample of the operating channel, and selects the maximum-a-posteriori coset (Secs.~\ref{sec:theory_structured} and~\ref{sec:methods_decoders}). It is Bayes-optimal for the structured channel [Eq.~\eqref{eq:map}] and provably reduces to the leader under i.i.d.\ noise, claiming no advantage where none exists. Its plug-in estimator is consistent in the calibration-sample size (Sec.~\ref{sec:theory_structured}), and the confidence it reports is the exact model posterior, calibrated by construction [Eq.~\eqref{eq:calib}]. We evaluate it inside one fully reproducible benchmark that holds together three properties usually kept apart: a single \texttt{decode(syndrome)\,$\to$\,(correction, confidence)} interface hosting all nine decoders across five regimes; selective decoding, so every decoder can abstain and we report risk--coverage curves rather than a single error number; and calibration as a reported metric. Every number carries an analytic $95\%$ confidence interval, and a code-level audit ties each reported value to a generated artifact and gates every superlative on statistical evidence.

This study is the thirteenth in a single-author program on spectral truncation of operators, the common thread being that a high-dimensional operator---an objective landscape, a product-formula residual, a transfer channel---is replaced by a low-rank surrogate retained on its dominant modes and used to commit, abstain, or transfer under a calibrated confidence. The earlier entries develop graph-conditioned trust regions and their uncertainty calibration for query-efficient QAOA~\cite{huynh2026gctr,huynh2026gctrcal,huynh2026gctrcalpro}, the measurement cost and certified query budgets of low-depth QAOA~\cite{huynh2026meascost,huynh2026certqb}, topology-conditioned QAOA parameter transfer and its operator-spectral truncated priors~\cite{huynh2026paramxfer,huynh2026paramxferB,huynh2026ostqaoa,huynh2026topoqaoa}, zero-overhead and Lie-algebraic spectral truncation of the Trotter error for Hamiltonian simulation~\cite{huynh2026topoham,huynh2026lie}, and a topological graph neural decoder for correlated quantum errors~\cite{huynh2026gnndec}; further entries in the program extend this thread through covariance-aware and gauge-equivariant decoders for correlated noise and directed spectral-truncated operators for quantum-phase classification~\cite{huynh2026covdec,huynh2026fiber,huynh2026dsto}. The present work carries that thread to the structured-noise decoding problem: the truncated transfer operator is the spectral surrogate, and its model posterior is the calibrated confidence that decides whether the decoder commits or abstains, exactly as calibration gates the commit-or-abstain decision throughout the program~\cite{huynh2026cptppinn,huynh2026drift}. Fixed-depth and representation-theoretic results for Hamiltonian simulation supply complementary context for the quantum circuits whose errors QEC must ultimately suppress~\cite{nguyen2025zassenhaus,jing2026complexity}.

The performance claim is single and precise. The spectral decoder reduces the logical error of the i.i.d.-optimal leader under each \emph{structured data-noise} regime---$\sync{44.0}\%$ under bias ($z=\sync{10.5}$), $\sync{33.0}\%$ under bursts ($z=\sync{16.6}$), and $\sync{21.8}\%$ under neighbour correlations ($z=\sync{11.6}$), pooling to $\sync{0.1291}$ against $\sync{0.1508}$--$\sync{0.1542}$ for all eight structure-agnostic decoders (Sec.~\ref{sec:headline}). The reduction vanishes exactly where the channel-matched optimum [Eq.~\eqref{eq:map}] forbids a single-shot advantage: the decoder ties the leader under i.i.d.\ noise ($z=\sync{2.0}$) and read-out noise ($z=\sync{0.3}$), where a corrupted syndrome carries no recoverable structure. The eight structure-agnostic decoders remain mutually indistinguishable on raw accuracy (spread $\sync{0.0033}$, below $1.5$ interval widths)---the original tie, now understood as a shared i.i.d.\ target rather than a saturation of the problem. Two further findings follow: abstention buys a large, monotone reduction in committed logical error (Sec.~\ref{sec:risk_coverage}), and calibration quality is not predicted by decoder sophistication, spanning a factor of nearly six across the agnostic family (Sec.~\ref{sec:calibration}).

\section{The structured-channel theory of decoding}
\label{sec:theory}

This section derives, from first principles, every structural fact the benchmark rests on: the algebra of the repetition syndrome and of logical failure; the i.i.d.\ optimality of the coset-leader (lookup) decoder and the syndrome-consistency of the heuristic decoders; their exact equivalence on the line, the analytic origin of the observed tie; the \emph{structured-channel gap} that makes the i.i.d.-optimal leader beatable, the transfer-operator decoder that closes it, and the posterior confidence that is calibrated by construction; the automorphism invariance of the logical error rate; the Bernoulli interval and indistinguishability criterion; and the calibration--selectivity guarantee behind the risk--coverage and expected-calibration-error metrics. The derivations are elementary and stay inline. They concern small, fully reproducible simulated benchmarks---a repetition code and a surface-like toy lattice under simulated structured noise---not hardware syndromes and not a full surface code at scale; the performance claim is a statistically evidenced advantage under \emph{simulated} structured noise (Sec.~\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{The repetition code: syndromes and logical failure}
\label{sec:theory_code}

We work over the binary field $\F_2=\{0,1\}$ with addition $\oplus$ (XOR) and Hamming weight $\abs{x}=\sum_i x_i$. Fix a distance $d\ge 2$. The distance-$d$ repetition code has data space $\F_2^d$ and parity-check matrix $H\in\F_2^{(d-1)\times d}$ with $(H_i)_i=(H_i)_{i+1}=1$ and all other entries zero, so the \emph{syndrome} of an error $e\in\F_2^d$ is
\begin{equation}
  s(e)=He, \qquad s_i(e)=e_i\oplus e_{i+1}, \quad i=0,\dots,d-2.
  \label{eq:syndrome}
\end{equation}
The \emph{logical functional} is $L(e)=\bigl(\sum_i e_i\bigr)\bmod 2$, the \emph{stabilizer group} is $\mathcal S=\ker H$, and a correction $\hat c$ \emph{succeeds} on error $e$ iff the residual $r=e\oplus\hat c$ satisfies $Hr=0$ and $L(r)=0$. The map $H$ is the boundary operator of the one-dimensional chain complex whose $1$-cells are the $d$ data bits and whose $0$-cells are the $d-1$ links.

Three elementary facts make the line picture exact. First, $Hx=0$ forces $x_i=x_{i+1}$ for all $i$, so the only solutions are the constants $0$ and $\mathbf 1=(1,\dots,1)$; hence
\begin{equation}
  \ker H=\mathcal S=\{0,\mathbf 1\}, \qquad \dim_{\F_2}\ker H=1,
  \label{eq:kernel}
\end{equation}
and by rank--nullity $\operatorname{rank}H=d-1$, so $H$ is surjective and every syndrome is realisable. Second, $s_i(e)=1$ exactly where $e_i\neq e_{i+1}$, i.e.\ at the boundaries between maximal constant runs of $e$; the lit checks $J=\{i:s_i=1\}$ therefore mark the \emph{endpoints} of the error chains, and $\abs J\equiv e_0\oplus e_{d-1}\pmod 2$ is even iff $e_0=e_{d-1}$. Decoding is thus a problem of pairing endpoints on a line---the picture every defect-graph decoder of Sec.~\ref{sec:methods_decoders} uses (Fig.~\ref{fig:schematic}).

Third, two errors share a syndrome iff they differ by a stabilizer: by Eq.~\eqref{eq:kernel}, $He=He'\iff e\oplus e'\in\ker H=\{0,\mathbf 1\}$. Each syndrome $s$ therefore labels a single \emph{coset}
\begin{equation}
  H^{-1}(s)=\{c_0,\,c_1\}, \qquad c_1=c_0\oplus\mathbf 1,
  \label{eq:coset}
\end{equation}
of size two, whose representatives carry opposite logical values $L(c_1)=L(c_0)\oplus(d\bmod 2)$ because $L$ is $\F_2$-linear with $L(\mathbf 1)=d\bmod 2$. The failure dichotomy follows: a correction with $H\hat c\neq s$ leaves $Hr\neq 0$ and fails outright, whereas a correction with $H\hat c=s$ leaves a stabilizer residual and succeeds iff $L(\hat c)=L(e)$, since $L(r)=L(e)\oplus L(\hat c)$. This is the exact statement of ``the code protected the bit'' that the runner implements.

\subsection{Decoder optimality and syndrome-consistency}
\label{sec:theory_decoders}

Fix the i.i.d.\ bit-flip channel at physical rate $p<\tfrac12$, so $\Pr(e)=p^{\abs e}(1-p)^{d-\abs e}=(1-p)^d\bigl(\tfrac{p}{1-p}\bigr)^{\abs e}$ is strictly decreasing in $\abs e$ because $0<p/(1-p)<1$. Among the errors consistent with an observed $s$ the most probable is then the lightest, so the \emph{coset leader} $\hat c=\arg\min_{e\in H^{-1}(s)}\abs e$ is the maximum-likelihood recovery. It is also Bayes-optimal for the logical bit: conditioned on $s$, the posterior of logical class $\ell$ is proportional to $\sum_{e\in H^{-1}(s),\,L(e)=\ell}\Pr(e)$, and since $\Pr(\cdot)$ decreases in weight the lighter representative of Eq.~\eqref{eq:coset} carries the more probable class---except on the measure-zero set where the leader has weight exactly $d/2$ and the two classes are equiprobable. The lookup decoder, which tabulates $\hat c$ per syndrome, therefore minimises the logical-failure probability on the i.i.d.\ channel, and is the yardstick of Table~\ref{tab:main}: no decoder can beat it there, which already caps how far any competitor's accuracy can sit from it.

The heuristic decoders never violate the basic syndrome constraint; each returns a member of $H^{-1}(s)$, so the only freedom left to it is the logical bit. \emph{Majority} integrates the syndrome by the cumulative XOR $c_j=\bigoplus_{i<j}s_i$, which satisfies $Hc=s$, and chooses the lighter of $\{c,c\oplus\mathbf 1\}$. \emph{Matching} pairs the lit checks $J$ and flips the bits between paired endpoints, reproducing exactly the toggles of $s$ (with a boundary closure when $\abs J$ is odd, which by the endpoint parity above corresponds to $e_0\neq e_{d-1}$). The \emph{neural}, \emph{topology}, and \emph{fused} decoders start from a syndrome-determined base chain and only ever add a stabilizer $\in\{0,\mathbf 1\}$ to pick the logical class, leaving $s$ unchanged since $H\mathbf 1=0$. By the failure dichotomy each therefore fails on a shot iff it selects the wrong logical class, never because of an unsatisfied syndrome. The neural decoder in particular reduces decoding to a single binary classification---the logical coset---which is why its confidence, a predicted class probability, is approximately calibrated (Sec.~\ref{sec:calibration}, Fig.~\ref{fig:risk_coverage}).

\subsection{Why the decoders tie: exact agreement on the line}
\label{sec:theory_tie}

On the line these rules coincide. Lookup, majority, and matching all return members of the same coset $H^{-1}(s)$, so only the choice between $c$ and $c\oplus\mathbf 1$ can differ. Lookup picks the lighter by definition; majority compares $\abs c$ with $\abs{c\oplus\mathbf 1}=d-\abs c$ and returns the lighter; and matching, pairing ordered endpoints without crossings, minimises the total flipped length $\sum_{\text{pairs}}(j_{\text{right}}-j_{\text{left}})$, which equals the Hamming weight of the produced chain---again the lighter representative. The three thus select the \emph{same} logical class for every syndrome except on the weight-$d/2$ tie set, where the two classes are equiprobable and any choice has equal conditional failure. Equal per-syndrome conditional failure gives an identical logical error rate on the i.i.d.\ channel.

This line-equivalence is the analytic origin of our first empirical finding: the eight structure-agnostic decoders are statistically indistinguishable on raw logical error (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Fig.~\ref{fig:threshold}). Up to a negligible tie set and finite-sample noise they are the \emph{same} decision rule, so their $\sync{0.0033}$ best-to-worst spread is residual sampling fluctuation, not signal. The equivalence also pinpoints the shared blind spot: all three optimise Hamming weight, which is the i.i.d.\ likelihood---exactly the assumption a structured channel violates.

\subsection{The structured-channel gap: why the i.i.d.-optimal leader is beatable}
\label{sec:theory_structured}

The premise of this work is that real noise is not i.i.d., and the gap this opens is sharp: wherever structure makes a heavier coset more probable than a lighter one---a set of syndromes of positive probability---the i.i.d.-optimal leader is strictly suboptimal, and that set is precisely the room available to beat it.

Let $P$ be any error distribution on $\F_2^d$. By Eq.~\eqref{eq:coset} each syndrome labels a two-element coset $\{c_0,c_1\}$ of opposite logical class, and a decoder returning a member leaves a stabilizer residual, failing iff it returns the member whose class differs from the truth. Conditioned on $s$, the success probability of returning $e$ is $P(e)/\bigl(P(c_0)+P(c_1)\bigr)$, so the rule that minimises logical failure on \emph{any} channel $P$ is the channel-matched maximum-a-posteriori (MAP) decoder
\begin{equation}
  \hat c(s)=\arg\max_{e\in\{c_0,c_1\}}P(e).
  \label{eq:map}
\end{equation}
For the i.i.d.\ channel $P(e)\propto(p/(1-p))^{\abs e}$ decreases in weight, so Eq.~\eqref{eq:map} returns the minimum-weight leader and reproduces the lookup optimum---it claims no advantage where none exists. When $P$ is spatially non-uniform or correlated, weight is no longer a sufficient statistic for $P$: on the set $\mathcal B$ of syndromes where the heavier representative is the more probable one, the leader picks the less probable class, and its logical-failure probability exceeds that of Eq.~\eqref{eq:map} by exactly
\begin{equation}
  \Delta=\sum_{s\in\mathcal B}\bigl|\,P(c_0^{(s)})-P(c_1^{(s)})\,\bigr|\;\ge 0,
  \label{eq:gap}
\end{equation}
the summed gap between the larger and smaller coset probabilities---the per-syndrome deficit $[\max-\min]/(P(c_0)+P(c_1))$ reweighted by $P(s)=P(c_0)+P(c_1)$---which is strictly positive whenever $\mathcal B$ carries mass.

Equation~\eqref{eq:gap} is the formal licence to beat the leader, but Eq.~\eqref{eq:map} is unusable without knowing $P$. We therefore model the channel by a tractable, learnable surrogate: a \emph{transfer operator}, the inhomogeneous first-order Markov (classical matrix-product) law
\begin{equation}
  P_\theta(e)=\pi_0(e_0)\prod_{i=0}^{d-2}T_i(e_i,e_{i+1}),
  \label{eq:transfer}
\end{equation}
specified by an initial law $\pi_0$ on $\F_2$ and per-site stochastic $2\times2$ transfer matrices $T_i$. Its log-likelihood $\log P_\theta(e)=\log\pi_0(e_0)+\sum_i\log T_i(e_i,e_{i+1})$ is a sum of local terms read along the chain, the defect-graph analogue of a length-$d$ matrix-product state whose dominant correlations live in the leading eigenmodes of the $T_i$. For a larger code one keeps the leading $k$ modes (the \emph{spectral truncation}); on the line each $T_i$ is already $2\times2$, so the representation is exact and no truncation is exercised by the experiments here---the truncation is the construction's generalisation to larger codes (Sec.~\ref{sec:limitations}).

When the true channel factorises as $P=P_\theta$, maximising $\log P_\theta$ over the two-element coset is identical to Eq.~\eqref{eq:map}, so the plug-in transfer-operator decoder---the \emph{spectral} decoder---is channel-matched optimal. It is also consistent: the Laplace-smoothed transition counts $\hat T_i(a,b)=\bigl(\eps+\#\{e:e_i=a,e_{i+1}=b\}\bigr)/(\text{row total})$ converge almost surely to $T_i(a,b)$ as the calibration sample grows (and likewise $\hat\pi_0\to\pi_0$), and continuity of the $\arg\max$ off the measure-zero tie set carries this through to the decision. The spectral decoder thus ties the leader under i.i.d.\ noise, where $\mathcal B$ is empty, and strictly beats it whenever $\mathcal B$ carries mass.

Its confidence is not a heuristic but the exact model posterior. Normalising the model probabilities over the two-element coset, the posterior of the chosen representative is $P_\theta(\hat c)/\bigl(P_\theta(c_0)+P_\theta(c_1)\bigr)$; with $\Delta\ell=\bigl|\log P_\theta(c_0)-\log P_\theta(c_1)\bigr|$, dividing numerator and denominator by $P_\theta(\hat c)$ gives
\begin{equation}
  C=\sigma(\Delta\ell)=\bigl(1+e^{-\Delta\ell}\bigr)^{-1}=P_\theta(\hat c\mid s),
  \label{eq:calib}
\end{equation}
the model posterior of the chosen coset. If $P_\theta$ equals the true channel then $\Pr(F=0\mid C)=C$ almost surely---the confidence is perfectly calibrated, with vanishing expected calibration error [Eq.~\eqref{eq:ece} below]---and the empirical shortfall of $C$ from this ideal is exactly model misspecification, largest under read-out noise, where the conditioned syndrome is itself corrupt. Equations~\eqref{eq:map}--\eqref{eq:calib} predict the experiments precisely: the spectral decoder must tie the leader under i.i.d.\ noise, strictly beat it whenever the data channel is biased or correlated, and be near-perfectly calibrated except where it conditions on a corrupted syndrome---exactly the pattern of Table~\ref{tab:spectral} and Fig.~\ref{fig:perregime}.

\subsection{Automorphism invariance of the logical error rate}
\label{sec:theory_auto}

The repetition code carries a cyclic (ring) symmetry, and a faithful decoder should respect it. For a shift $t$ let $\rho_t$ be the cyclic relabeling $(\rho_t e)_i=e_{(i-t)\bmod d}$, and call a decoder $\calD$ cyclically equivariant if $\calD(s(\rho_t e))=\rho_t(\calD(s(e)))$. The logical functional is shift-invariant, $L(\rho_t e)=L(e)$, and the shift commutes with the check structure, so for an equivariant decoder the residual transforms as $\rho_t e\oplus\calD(s(\rho_t e))=\rho_t\bigl(e\oplus\calD(s(e))\bigr)$; the shifted residual is a zero-logical-value stabilizer iff the original is, hence the failure indicator obeys $F(\rho_t e)=F(e)$ pointwise. If the noise is cyclically invariant, $\Pr(\rho_t e)=\Pr(e)$ (as the i.i.d.\ channel is), then taking expectations gives $\Pr[\calD\text{ fails on }\rho_t e]=\Pr[\calD\text{ fails on }e]$: the logical error rate is invariant under relabeling. The matching decoder is equivariant up to the boundary-closure convention, so its rate is invariant under shifts $\{1,2,d/2\}$ to within Monte-Carlo tolerance; the run reports this as the \texttt{automorphism\_invariant} flag, which is \sync{true} at reported scale (Sec.~\ref{sec:per_regime}).

\subsection{Honest intervals and statistical indistinguishability}
\label{sec:theory_ci}

Every reported rate is a mean of $n$ i.i.d.\ Bernoulli failure indicators $F_k$, so $\hat p=\frac1n\sum_k F_k$ has $\mathbb E[\hat p]=p_L$ and $\operatorname{Var}(\hat p)=p_L(1-p_L)/n$; the central limit theorem together with Slutsky's theorem makes
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{ci}_{95}=1.96\sqrt{\hat p(1-\hat p)/n}
  \label{eq:ci}
\end{equation}
a consistent estimate of the $95\%$ interval half-width. Two independent rates differing by $\abs{\hat p_A-\hat p_B}\le\mathrm{ci}_{95}$ are not separated at $95\%$: their difference has standard error at most $\sqrt 2\,\mathrm{ci}_{95}/1.96$, so a gap within one $\mathrm{ci}_{95}$ lies inside the two-sided acceptance region for equality. At $n=\sync{100{,}000}$ pooled shots the eight structure-agnostic decoders span $\sync{0.1508}$--$\sync{0.1542}$ with $\mathrm{ci}_{95}\approx\sync{0.0022}$, so their $\sync{0.0033}$ spread is below $1.5$ interval widths and they are statistically tied (Table~\ref{tab:main})---exactly the regime the line-equivalence of Sec.~\ref{sec:theory_tie} predicts.

\subsection{Calibration gates selective decoding}
\label{sec:theory_selective}

The risk--coverage view carries a decision-theoretic guarantee when the confidence is calibrated, which is why we report calibration alongside the error rate. A confidence $C\in[0,1]$ is \emph{perfectly calibrated} for the failure indicator $F$ if $\Pr(F=0\mid C=c)=c$ almost everywhere, and its deviation from calibration is the expected calibration error
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{ECE}=\sum_{b=1}^{B}\frac{n_b}{n}\,\bigl\lvert\overline{\mathrm{conf}}_b-\overline{\mathrm{acc}}_b\bigr\rvert,
  \label{eq:ece}
\end{equation}
with $\overline{\mathrm{conf}}_b$ and $\overline{\mathrm{acc}}_b=1-\overline F_b$ the mean confidence and accuracy in bin $b$ and $n_b$ the bin count. Under perfect calibration the tower property gives $\Pr(F=0\mid C\in b)=\mathbb E[C\mid C\in b]$ for every bin, so $\mathrm{ECE}=0$: ``when the decoder says $0.8$ it is right $80\%$ of the time'' is exactly $\mathrm{ECE}=0$. This is the identity invoked by Eq.~\eqref{eq:calib}. Empirically ECE spans a factor of nearly six across the structure-agnostic family ($\sync{0.0446}$--$\sync{0.2624}$) and is uncorrelated with sophistication; the spectral decoder's posterior confidence drives its ECE near zero where its model matches the channel (Sec.~\ref{sec:calibration}).

Calibration also makes selective decoding monotone. With committed risk $R(\tau)=\Pr(F=1\mid C\ge\tau)$ and coverage $G(\tau)=\Pr(C\ge\tau)$, perfect calibration gives $\Pr(F=1\mid C)=1-C$, so
\begin{equation}
  R(\tau)=1-\mathbb E[C\mid C\ge\tau].
  \label{eq:risk}
\end{equation}
Raising $\tau$ shrinks the committed set, so $G$ is non-increasing; and $\mathbb E[C\mid C\ge\tau]$ is non-decreasing in $\tau$, because the mean of $C$ over $\{C\ge\tau_1\}$ is an occupancy-weighted average of its means over $\{C\ge\tau_2\}$ and $\{\tau_1\le C<\tau_2\}$, the latter strictly smaller. Hence $R$ falls as coverage shrinks. We do not assume Eq.~\eqref{eq:risk} in the experiments; the empirical confidences are imperfect, so the risk--coverage curves and ECE quantify how far each decoder falls from the ideal. The predicted monotone fall is what Fig.~\ref{fig:risk_coverage} and the runner's monotonicity check verify: matching's committed error drops from $\sync{0.1525}$ at full coverage to $\sync{0.0108}$ at $\sync{0.25}$ coverage (Sec.~\ref{sec:risk_coverage}).

