\subsection{Benchmark design}
\label{sec:design}

Figure~\ref{fig:schematic} summarises the end-to-end pipeline: structured noise corrupts a code word, the syndrome on the defect graph is passed to the shared decoder interface, a confidence threshold gates abstention, and the logical error rate, calibration, and risk--coverage metrics are scored with $95\%$ confidence intervals.

\textbf{Codes and defect graphs.} The primary benchmark is the bit-flip repetition code: $d$ data bits over $\F_2$ with $d-1$ weight-two parity checks $Z_iZ_{i+1}$. A syndrome bit $s_i=e_i\oplus e_{i+1}$ fires where an error chain crosses link $i$, so defects live on a one-dimensional line and decoding is a matching problem on that line. The single logical observable is the global parity, and a correction succeeds iff the residual $e\oplus\hat c$ is a stabilizer. A second code---a $d\times d$ surface-like toy lattice with plaquette defects matched by Manhattan distance---exposes the same defect geometry in two dimensions; it is a topology-faithful proxy, not a stabilizer-exact surface code. Both codes expose their Tanner graph, so the matching, topology, and BP decoders operate on the code topology directly.

\textbf{Structured-noise regimes.} Five seeded samplers define the regimes (Sec.~\ref{sec:methods_noise}): \emph{i.i.d.}\ (each bit flips independently at rate $p$); \emph{biased} (interleaved sublattices flip at $p$ and $\mathrm{bias}\cdot p$, a coarse Pauli-bias surrogate); \emph{burst} (contiguous correlated runs of flips); \emph{correlated} (a seed flip recruits its lattice neighbours); and \emph{measurement\_error} (i.i.d.\ data noise plus per-check syndrome read-out flips). These are precisely the regimes where topology-awareness is hypothesised to help over a memoryless majority vote.

\textbf{Decoder family.} All nine decoders share one interface returning a correction and a confidence in $[0,1]$ (Sec.~\ref{sec:methods_decoders}). Eight are \emph{structure-agnostic}---each targets the i.i.d.-optimal coset leader: \texttt{lookup} (the optimal minimum-weight leader, the gold standard at small $d$); \texttt{majority} (integrate the syndrome and majority-vote); \texttt{matching} (minimum-weight pairing of defects---endpoint pairing on the line, \texttt{max\_weight\_matching} on the surface graph); \texttt{bp} (a few rounds of min-sum message passing on the Tanner graph, deferring to matching when it fails to satisfy the syndrome); \texttt{neural} (a logistic-regression coset selector trained on sampled syndromes); \texttt{topology} (matching recalibrated by defect-geometry features); \texttt{fused} (matching $+$ neural combined by confidence); and \texttt{rl} (a contextual bandit that, per syndrome context, selects which decoder to trust). The ninth is the channel-adaptive \texttt{spectral} decoder (Sec.~\ref{sec:theory_structured}): it estimates the truncated transfer operator $P_\theta(e)=\pi_0(e_0)\prod_i T_i(e_i,e_{i+1})$ from a small calibration sample of the operating channel and returns the maximum-a-posteriori coset, with confidence equal to the model posterior of that coset.

\textbf{Metrics.} For each (regime, decoder) pair we Monte-Carlo many shots and record, per shot, the logical-failure indicator and the decoder's confidence. From these we compute the logical error rate (with analytic $95\%$ confidence interval $1.96\,\hat\sigma/\sqrt n$), the expected calibration error between stated confidence and realised accuracy over ten confidence bins, the coverage at a fixed abstention threshold, and the full risk--coverage curve obtained by sweeping that threshold. An automorphism check---invariance of the logical error rate under cyclic relabeling---serves as an integrity flag.

\subsection{Breaking the decoder tie: the spectral decoder wins under structured noise}
\label{sec:headline}

Table~\ref{tab:main} reports the headline comparison at physical error rate $p=\sync{0.12}$, pooled over the five regimes ($n=\sync{100{,}000}$ shots per decoder at reported scale: distance $\sync{9}$, $\sync{20{,}000}$ trials per regime). The eight structure-agnostic decoders occupy a narrow band---from $\sync{0.1508}$ (majority) to $\sync{0.1542}$ (lookup)---with every $95\%$ confidence interval $\approx\pm\sync{0.0022}$; their $\sync{0.0033}$ best-to-worst spread is below $1.5$ interval widths, so they are mutually indistinguishable on raw logical error, exactly as the line-equivalence of Sec.~\ref{sec:theory_tie} predicts for decoders that all target the i.i.d.\ leader. The channel-adaptive \texttt{spectral} decoder sits well outside that band: its pooled logical error is $\sync{0.1291}$, lower than the best agnostic decoder by $\sync{0.0217}$---roughly ten $95\%$ interval widths, a separation no finite-sample fluctuation can produce. It is the first decoder in the family to leave the tie.

The advantage is not uniform, and it should not be: it concentrates exactly where the structured-channel gap [Eq.~\eqref{eq:gap}] makes the leader suboptimal. Table~\ref{tab:spectral} and Fig.~\ref{fig:perregime} resolve the pooled number by regime. Against the i.i.d.-optimal leader, the spectral decoder cuts the logical error by $\sync{44.0}\%$ under bias ($z=\sync{10.5}$), $\sync{33.0}\%$ under bursts ($z=\sync{16.6}$), and $\sync{21.8}\%$ under neighbour correlations ($z=\sync{11.6}$)---all significant at $z>10$. In the two regimes where no single-shot data-channel advantage exists it does \emph{not} win: under i.i.d.\ noise the difference is $\sync{0.00085}$ ($z=\sync{2.0}$, below the audited $z>3$ significance threshold), and under read-out noise it is $\sync{0.00150}$ ($z=\sync{0.3}$, not significant)---a corrupted syndrome carries no recoverable structure for a single-shot decoder. The threshold curves of Fig.~\ref{fig:threshold} confirm the i.i.d.\ tie: across the physical-error grid the spectral curve overlaps the agnostic decoders and the optimal lookup leader, all staying below the break-even line $p_L=p$ (at $p=\sync{0.21}$ the logical error is only $\approx\sync{0.024}$ for every core decoder). The win is a structured-noise phenomenon, and the benchmark localises it.

