\subsection{Calibration is not predicted by decoder sophistication}
\label{sec:calibration}

The confidence that gates abstention is useful only if it is calibrated, and the benchmark returns two calibration findings---one preserved, one new. First, across the eight structure-agnostic decoders, calibration is essentially uncorrelated with sophistication and not predictable from accuracy: pooled ECE spans a factor of nearly six, $\texttt{neural}\ \sync{0.0446} < \texttt{majority}\ \sync{0.0498} < \texttt{lookup}\ \sync{0.0558} < \texttt{fused}\ \sync{0.0622} < \texttt{bp}\ \sync{0.1238} < \texttt{topology}\ \sync{0.2421} < \texttt{matching}\ \sync{0.2620} < \texttt{rl}\ \sync{0.2624}$, even though all eight are tied on logical error. Plain minimum-weight \texttt{matching}, whose defect-fraction confidence tracks accuracy poorly, is among the worst; the lightweight logistic-regression \texttt{neural} selector, whose confidence is a predicted-class probability, is the best of the agnostic family.

The second finding is the empirical face of Eq.~\eqref{eq:calib}: the spectral decoder's confidence is the \emph{exact model posterior} of the chosen coset, so it is near-perfectly calibrated wherever the transfer operator matches the channel. The per-regime ECE (Table~\ref{tab:ed_ece}) bears this out precisely: spectral is the best-calibrated decoder under i.i.d.\ noise (ECE $\sync{0.00069}$), bias ($\sync{0.00159}$), and bursts ($\sync{0.02538}$)---one to three orders of magnitude below the agnostic decoders there---and nearly so under correlations ($\sync{0.04859}$, just behind lookup's $\sync{0.04465}$). The single exception is the read-out regime, where its ECE jumps to $\sync{0.32359}$: there the decoder is confident in a posterior computed from a \emph{corrupted} syndrome, so it is over-confident exactly as Eq.~\eqref{eq:calib} predicts under model misspecification. This one regime inflates its pooled ECE to $\sync{0.0629}$ (Table~\ref{tab:main}), competitive with but not better than the best agnostic decoders---a faithful summary that the per-regime view sharpens rather than hides. Treating calibration as a first-class, regime-resolved metric is what makes this visible: a deployment that can characterise its channel gets, for free, a decoder whose abstention is trustworthy precisely where its accuracy gains are real.

\subsection{Structured noise separates regimes, not decoders}
\label{sec:per_regime}

The per-(regime, decoder) breakdown (Table~\ref{tab:ed_ler}; Sec.~\ref{sec:methods_perregime}) shows the noise regime to be the dominant axis of \emph{difficulty}: the agnostic decoders' logical error rises from $\approx\sync{0.002}$ under i.i.d.\ noise to $\approx\sync{0.043}$ (biased), $\approx\sync{0.17}$ (burst), $\approx\sync{0.21}$ (correlated), and $\approx\sync{0.34}$ (measurement error)---a more than hundredfold change driven entirely by structure, with read-out noise by far the hardest. Within each regime the eight agnostic decoders cluster inside their confidence intervals (no single one is significantly best), exactly as the line-equivalence (Sec.~\ref{sec:theory_tie}) predicts. The spectral decoder is the lone exception: it posts the lowest entry in the i.i.d., biased, burst, and correlated columns ($\sync{0.00145}$, $\sync{0.02400}$, $\sync{0.11960}$, $\sync{0.16190}$), but it pulls \emph{statistically} clear of the cluster only on the three structured data-noise columns, by margins of $z\ge\sync{10.5}$ (Table~\ref{tab:spectral}); under i.i.d.\ noise its $\sync{0.00085}$ edge over the provably optimal leader is within sampling error ($z=\sync{2.0}$), exactly as the i.i.d.\ optimality of the leader [Eq.~\eqref{eq:map}] requires. It falls back \emph{into} the cluster under read-out noise ($\sync{0.33870}$, statistically tied with the leader's $\sync{0.34020}$), where the corrupted syndrome leaves no data-channel structure to exploit. The integrity flag \texttt{automorphism\_invariant} is \sync{true}, confirming the matching decoder respects the code's cyclic symmetry to within statistical tolerance.

