\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

In this work, we introduced the channel-adaptive spectral decoder, a truncated transfer operator on the defect graph that is the first member of a nine-decoder family to leave the accuracy tie---and it does so by exactly the mechanism the theory isolates. Three findings follow. First, the eight structure-agnostic decoders are tied on raw logical error not because the problem is saturated but because they share one i.i.d.\ target (the line-equivalence, Sec.~\ref{sec:theory_tie}); the moment a decoder models the channel [Eq.~\eqref{eq:map}], the tie breaks, by $20$--$44\%$ on the structured data-noise regimes ($z>10$). Second, the win is sharply localised and honestly bounded: it vanishes under i.i.d.\ noise and under read-out noise, where the channel-matched optimum [Eq.~\eqref{eq:map}] permits no data-channel advantage, and the benchmark's per-regime $z$-scores make that boundary visible rather than averaging it away. Third, calibration is a structural, regime-dependent property: across the agnostic family it spans nearly sixfold and is uncorrelated with sophistication, while the spectral decoder's posterior confidence is provably (Eq.~\eqref{eq:calib}) and empirically near-perfectly calibrated wherever its model matches the channel, degrading only where it conditions on a corrupted syndrome. Selectivity and calibration as first-class metrics are what render all three visible at once.

\textbf{Relation to prior work.} Matching~\cite{dennis2002topological,fowler2012surface,higgott2022pymatching}, belief propagation~\cite{poulin2008iterative,roffe2020decoding}, and neural and reinforcement-learning decoders~\cite{torlai2017neural,varsamopoulos2017decoding,sweke2020reinforcement,bausch2024learning} are each studied in depth, typically optimising the logical error rate. The idea that a decoder should match the \emph{noise model}---weighted or correlated matching, soft and belief-matching---is well established for surface codes~\cite{higgott2022pymatching,roffe2020decoding}; our contribution is to cast it as a \emph{truncated transfer operator on the defect graph} with provable channel-matched optimality [Eqs.~\eqref{eq:map} and~\eqref{eq:transfer}] and a posterior confidence that is calibrated by construction [Eq.~\eqref{eq:calib}], and to embed it in a benchmark that imports selective prediction~\cite{chow1970optimum,geifman2017selective,elyaniv2010foundations} and calibration~\cite{guo2017calibration} from machine learning as first-class axes. The performance claim is deliberately scoped: a statistically significant advantage under \emph{simulated} structured noise on toy codes. Where the literature reports learned decoders exceeding matching on hardware syndromes~\cite{bausch2024learning}, we do not reproduce that regime; our point is that channel-awareness, not algorithmic sophistication within the i.i.d.-optimal class, is the lever.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item \textbf{Toy codes only.} The primary benchmark is a one-dimensional repetition code; the second is a surface-\emph{like} toy lattice, not a stabilizer-exact CSS surface code, and we do not evaluate a full surface code at scale. The repetition code's binary coset and line geometry are exactly what make the agnostic decoders coincide; on a 2D code the transfer operator is higher-dimensional and the spectral truncation [Eq.~\eqref{eq:transfer}] becomes a genuine approximation we have not yet stress-tested.
    \item \textbf{Simulated structured noise.} All noise is produced by parametric samplers, not measured from hardware. The biased, burst, correlated, and measurement-error regimes are plausible surrogates, but real device noise has correlations (leakage, crosstalk, drift) that our first-order Markov model and our samplers do not capture.
    \item \textbf{The advantage requires a calibration sample.} The spectral decoder is \emph{channel-adaptive}: it must be matched to the operating channel from a labelled calibration sample (here $\sync{12{,}000}$ shots, the same data the neural decoder trains on). Where the channel is unknown, drifting, or uncharacterisable, the advantage shrinks toward the i.i.d.\ tie; an online or unsupervised estimator of the transfer operator is future work.
    \item \textbf{Single-distance focus.} The headline is at one distance and one physical error rate; the threshold curves sweep $p$ at fixed distance, but we do not present a distance-scaling (sub-threshold) study, the decisive test for a true topological code.
    \item \textbf{First-order model.} The transfer operator is a first-order (nearest-neighbour) Markov chain; longer-range correlations would require higher-order terms or a wider spectral truncation. The read-out regime exposes the model's blind spot: it cannot help when the conditioned syndrome is itself corrupt.
    \item \textbf{Solo author, single environment.} All results were produced and audited by one author on a single CPU platform; independent replication on other hardware and by other groups remains to be done.
\end{enumerate}

A deployable QEC decoder must do more than minimise an i.i.d.\ logical error rate: it must exploit the structure of the noise, know when to abstain, and report calibrated confidence. We showed that the textbook and learned decoders are statistically tied precisely because they all optimise the i.i.d.\ likelihood, and we introduced a channel-adaptive spectral decoder---a truncated transfer operator on the defect graph---that is Bayes-optimal for the structured channel, breaks the tie by $20$--$44\%$ under biased, bursty, and correlated noise ($z>10$), ties honestly where no advantage is possible, and reports a posterior confidence that is calibrated by construction. The most actionable message is that channel-awareness, not algorithmic sophistication within the i.i.d.-optimal class, is what separates decoders under realistic noise---and that it can be made provable, tractable, and self-calibrating. Extending the transfer-operator decoder to a full surface code at scale, to higher-order and online-estimated channel models, to hardware syndromes, and to a distance-scaling study---while keeping selectivity and calibration first-class---is the natural next step.

\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Codes}
\label{sec:methods_codes}
The repetition code (\texttt{RepetitionCode}) has $n=d$ data bits and $d-1$ weight-two checks $Z_iZ_{i+1}$; the syndrome of an error $e\in\F_2^d$ is $s_i=e_i\oplus e_{i+1}$. The logical observable is the global parity $\sum_i e_i \bmod 2$. A correction $\hat c$ is counted as a logical failure unless the residual $r=e\oplus\hat c$ has zero syndrome (is a stabilizer) and zero logical value; a residual that does not even reproduce the syndrome is also a failure. The lookup decoder enumerates all $2^d$ errors to tabulate the minimum-weight leader per syndrome. The surface-like code (\texttt{SurfaceLikeCode}) places $d^2$ data bits on a grid with $(d-1)^2$ plaquette (weight-four) checks; defects are paired by Manhattan-distance matching and the logical observable is the parity of the left column. Both codes expose a bipartite Tanner graph via \texttt{networkx}~\cite{hagberg2008exploring}.

\subsection{Structured-noise samplers}
\label{sec:methods_noise}
Each sampler takes a seeded \texttt{numpy} generator~\cite{harris2020array} and returns a $0/1$ error vector. \emph{i.i.d.}: each bit flips at rate $p$. \emph{biased}: even-index bits flip at $\min(1,\mathrm{bias}\cdot p)$ and odd-index bits at $p$ (default $\mathrm{bias}=3$). \emph{burst}: scanning the line, with probability $p$ a contiguous run of length $1+\mathrm{Poisson}(\mathrm{burst\_len}-1)$ is flipped (default $\mathrm{burst\_len}=3$). \emph{correlated}: an i.i.d.\ seed pattern recruits each neighbour with probability $\mathrm{corr}$ (default $0.5$) over two sweeps. \emph{measurement\_error}: i.i.d.\ data noise at rate $p$ plus a per-check syndrome-flip mask at rate $q$ (default $0.05$), which the runner XORs into the measured syndrome.

\subsection{Decoders}
\label{sec:methods_decoders}
All nine decoders implement \texttt{decode(syndrome)\,$\to$\,(correction, confidence)}; eight are structure-agnostic (they target the i.i.d.\ leader) and the ninth (\emph{Spectral}) is channel-adaptive. \emph{Lookup}: a table from syndrome to minimum-weight leader; confidence boosted toward $1$ since the leader is exact. \emph{Majority}: cumulative XOR of the syndrome recovers a flip chain up to a global offset, choosing the lower-weight coset representative; confidence is the normalised majority margin. \emph{Matching}: on the line, defects are paired left-to-right with boundary closure for an odd defect, returning the lighter coset; on the surface, a complete graph on lit plaquettes weighted by negative Manhattan distance is solved with \texttt{max\_weight\_matching}~\cite{edmonds1965paths,hagberg2008exploring} and an L-shaped path is flipped between matched pairs. Confidence is a defect-fraction heuristic $\exp(-2\,n_{\mathrm{def}}/n_{\mathrm{checks}})$, damped when a defect is unpaired. \emph{BP}: a few rounds of min-sum message passing on the Tanner graph with log-likelihood priors; if the thresholded beliefs do not reproduce the syndrome, it defers to matching. \emph{Neural}: a two-stage learned coset selector---a syndrome-determined base chain plus a \texttt{scikit-learn} logistic-regression classifier~\cite{pedregosa2011scikit} predicting the logical coset of the true correction relative to that chain; the output is always syndrome-consistent and confidence is the predicted-class probability. \emph{Topology}: matching with confidence recalibrated by defect-geometry features (count, span, gap structure). \emph{Fused}: matching and neural both propose; agreement boosts confidence, disagreement defers to the more confident proposal with damped confidence. \emph{RL}: a contextual bandit~\cite{li2010contextual,sutton2018reinforcement} whose context is a coarse defect-count bucket and whose actions are candidate decoders; it keeps per-context running success estimates, plays $\eps$-greedy during a warm-up stream, and at evaluation selects the highest-value decoder per context. \emph{Spectral} (\texttt{SpectralDecoder}): the channel-adaptive contribution. From a calibration sample $\{e^{(j)}\}$ of the operating channel it estimates a truncated transfer operator (Eq.~\eqref{eq:transfer})---an initial law $\hat\pi_0$ and per-site $2\times2$ transition matrices $\hat T_i$, each by normalised, Laplace-smoothed counts. Given a syndrome it forms the two coset representatives $\{c_0,c_0\oplus\mathbf 1\}$ (the matching base chain and its logical partner) and returns the one of larger model log-likelihood $\log\hat\pi_0(e_0)+\sum_i\log\hat T_i(e_i,e_{i+1})$; the confidence is the exact model posterior $\sigma(|\Delta\ell|)$ of the chosen coset. On the surface-like code the operator degrades to a per-bit field model (Bayes-optimal for the inhomogeneous-marginal channel). The decoder is Bayes-optimal for a Markov channel (Eq.~\eqref{eq:map}) and calibrated by construction (Eq.~\eqref{eq:calib}).

\subsection{Metrics and confidence intervals}
\label{sec:methods_metrics}
The logical error rate is the mean of per-shot failure indicators; its $95\%$ confidence interval is $1.96\,\hat\sigma/\sqrt n$ with $\hat\sigma$ the sample standard deviation (Bernoulli, so $\hat\sigma\approx\sqrt{\bar p(1-\bar p)}$). The expected calibration error~\cite{guo2017calibration,naeini2015obtaining} partitions shots into ten equal-width confidence bins and averages $\abs{\text{(mean confidence)}-\text{(mean accuracy)}}$ weighted by bin occupancy, where accuracy is $1-$failure. The risk--coverage curve sweeps an abstention threshold from the minimum to the maximum observed confidence; at each threshold, coverage is the committed fraction and risk is the logical error rate on committed shots. The curve is reported in descending-coverage order and a monotonicity check verifies that risk is (weakly) non-increasing as coverage shrinks.

\subsection{Integrity: automorphism invariance and the audit gate}
\label{sec:methods_audit}
The repetition code is invariant under cyclic relabeling of its data bits (ring symmetry); a faithful decoder must yield the same logical error rate on cyclically rotated errors. The runner checks this for the matching decoder over shifts $\{1,2,d/2\}$ within a statistical tolerance and exposes it as the \texttt{automorphism\_invariant} flag. A separate audit module enforces that every reported number traces to a value in the generated \texttt{summary.json} (the single source of truth), requires the central spectral-versus-leader claim to be statistically evidenced (per-regime $z$-scores), and rejects unevidenced superlatives; this discipline runs in continuous integration.

\subsection{Experimental protocol}
\label{sec:methods_protocol}
A run builds the code, fits the neural decoder on a mixed-regime training stream, and warms up the RL bandit on its own Monte-Carlo stream. Then, for each (regime, decoder), it Monte-Carlos \texttt{trials} shots at the evaluation physical error rate $p_{\mathrm{eval}}$, records the per-shot failure and confidence, and aggregates the metrics above. The channel-adaptive step is here: immediately before each regime is evaluated, the spectral decoder's transfer operator is re-estimated from that regime's calibration sample (the per-regime slice of the training stream---the same labelled data the neural decoder sees, so the comparison is fair). Threshold curves sweep $p$ for the seven core decoders under i.i.d.\ noise, with the spectral decoder calibrated on the i.i.d.\ stream (where it provably ties the leader); the risk--coverage curve is built from the spectral and matching decoders' pooled confidences. Seeds, platform, library versions, runtime, and peak memory are recorded as provenance in \texttt{summary.json}. All numbers in this paper are from the \emph{reported-scale} configuration: distance $\sync{9}$, $\sync{20{,}000}$ trials per (regime, decoder) ($\sync{100{,}000}$ pooled shots per decoder), $\sync{12{,}000}$ training/calibration shots, $p_{\mathrm{eval}}=\sync{0.12}$, an eleven-point physical-error grid, run end-to-end in $\sync{136}$\,s at $\sync{262}$\,MB peak memory on a single CPU. A faster smoke configuration (distance $\sync{5}$, $\sync{1{,}500}$ trials, $\approx\sync{10}$\,s) is provided for continuous integration.

\textbf{Statistics.} Each reported logical error rate is a mean of $n$ independent Bernoulli failure indicators; the $\pm95\%$ interval is the analytic Wald half-width $1.96\sqrt{\hat p(1-\hat p)/n}$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:ci}). Two decoders are called statistically indistinguishable when their means differ by less than this half-width, and a difference is called significant when the two-sample $z=(\hat p_A-\hat p_B)/\sqrt{\hat p_A(1-\hat p_A)/n+\hat p_B(1-\hat p_B)/n}$ exceeds $3$. This $z>3$ rule is the operative, audited significance threshold throughout, deliberately more conservative than the $95\%$ two-sample test of Eq.~\eqref{eq:ci} ($z>1.96$); a nominal $\sim\!2\sigma$ gap such as the i.i.d.\ spectral-versus-leader difference ($z=\sync{2.0}$) is therefore reported as a tie. The per-regime spectral-versus-leader $z$-scores are written to \texttt{summary.json} and audited. The neural classifier's training stream ($\sync{12{,}000}$ shots) is generated independently of the evaluation stream; all randomness derives from a single master seed (\texttt{seed}~$=\sync{0}$). The exact run provenance---platform \texttt{macOS-26.5.1-arm64}, \texttt{python}~\sync{3.13.12}, \texttt{numpy}~\sync{2.4.3}, \texttt{scipy}~\sync{1.17.1}, \texttt{networkx}~\sync{3.6.1}, \texttt{scikit-learn}~\sync{1.8.0}, \texttt{matplotlib}~\sync{3.10.8}---is stored alongside the results. Software: \texttt{numpy}~\cite{harris2020array}, \texttt{scipy}~\cite{virtanen2020scipy}, \texttt{networkx}~\cite{hagberg2008exploring}, \texttt{scikit-learn}~\cite{pedregosa2011scikit}, \texttt{matplotlib}~\cite{hunter2007matplotlib}; CPU only.

\subsection{Per-regime results}
\label{sec:methods_perregime}
The per-(regime, decoder) logical error rate, ECE, and coverage at $p_{\mathrm{eval}}$ are written in full to \texttt{summary.json} under \texttt{by\_regime}, and the per-regime spectral-versus-leader reductions and $z$-scores under \texttt{headline.spectral\_by\_regime}. At reported scale the qualitative pattern is regime-dominated for the agnostic decoders: i.i.d.\ noise yields $\approx\sync{0.002}$ logical error; biased $\approx\sync{0.043}$; burst $\approx\sync{0.17}$; correlated $\approx\sync{0.21}$; measurement-error $\approx\sync{0.34}$ (the hardest regime, since syndrome read-out flips corrupt the very signal the decoders condition on). The spectral decoder departs from this cluster on the three structured data-noise regimes ($\sync{0.024}$ biased, $\sync{0.120}$ burst, $\sync{0.162}$ correlated) and rejoins it under i.i.d.\ and read-out noise. Coverage varies strongly by decoder: matching's coverage at the fixed operating threshold ranges from $\sync{0.28}$ (biased) to $\sync{0.67}$ (i.i.d.); topology and the RL selector also abstain substantially; spectral, majority, lookup, neural, and fused commit fully (coverage $1.0$) in every regime.

